[38;5;231m⏺[39m I'm treating this as a bounded change. It touches the existing page ([38;5;153mactivity.html[39m, [38;5;153mactivity.js[39m, [38;5;153mactivity.css[39m) and
  nothing new structurally, so I'll put a short design here in chat and not write a spec. Tell me if you think it needs
  more than that.

  The first question is where the filter controls should go, which is easier to show than describe. I've opened a
  browser companion at:

  [94m]8;id=1kejt6b;http://localhost:63589/?key=214c2970d624b2f1e539f3d03d9754c31e97e0835e5a1e939cab3897ba46d152\http://localhost:63589/?key=214c2970d624b2f1e539f3d03d9754c31e97e0835e5a1e939cab3897ba46d152[39m]8;;\

  It shows three rough layouts. Each one keeps the table as it is and adds a "Showing N of M events" status line:

  - [1mA, filter bar:[0m always visible above the table. It has an event type dropdown, a search box, and a "Show failed
    sign-ins" shortcut.
  - [1mB, type chips:[0m one toggle for each of the nine event types, with "Sign-in failed" first. These wrap to several lines
    on narrow screens.
  - [1mC, Filter button with a popover:[0m keeps the header compact and shows active filters as removable tags. Filtering
    takes one more click.

  I'd go with A. Security wants failed sign-ins easy to find, and the only support tickets came from people looking for
  a failed sign-in they didn't recognize. A puts that shortcut where everyone sees it without opening anything. It also
  works well at 320px width.

  Click an option in the browser if you like, but reply here so I know you've decided. The labels and controls in the
  sketches are placeholders for now; we'll settle which filters to include next.

  Two notes:
  - The companion uses a lot of tokens. I'll close it once we're past the visual questions.
  - It saves files under [38;5;153m.hyperpowers/[39m, which isn't in [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m yet. You may want to add it so it can't get committed
    by accident.

